        <p>THEORETICAL AND HISTORICAL PERSPECTIVES</p>
        <p>EDITED BY <span data-field="editor_name">{{ editor_name }}</span>, <span data-field="editor_name_2">{{ editor_name_2 }}</span> AND <span data-field="editor_name_3">{{ editor_name_3 }}</span></p>
        <p>Asian regional integration has shown remarkable progress since the end of the 1990s, but is currently at a major crossroads amid confusion of debate over the direction and effectiveness of integration, and friction brought about by the rise of non-state actors, the 'inter-nationalization' of internal problems, and the 'internationalization' of internal problems. Signs of instability of the global economy are increasingly complex and multilayered. The challenges of regional integration in East Asia are increasingly complex and multilayered. The challenges of regional integration in East Asia are increasingly complex and multilayered.</p>
        <p><strong>Regional Integration in East Asia</strong> is the culmination of five years of intensive research on Asian regional integration by Waseda University. It re-examines theoretical approaches to comprehending the actual state of integration in the region and presents the most compelling among them. It also examines the state of regional cooperation in Asia in areas ranging from the economy to energy and the environment, security and education. In addition, the book offers long-term historical perspectives on the process of regional integration of Japan, ASEAN and China, on Asian regional integration. Thus, it sheds light on diverse aspects of regional integration in East Asia and offers a comprehensive portrayal of the actual condition of Asia and the state of endeavours to build regional cooperation and institutions.</p>
        <p><strong>Satoshi Amako</strong> is a Professor at the Graduate School of Asia-Pacific Studies, Waseda University, Japan. <strong>Shunji Matsuoka</strong> is a Professor at the Graduate School of Asia-Pacific Studies, Waseda University, Japan. <strong>Kenji Horiuchi</strong> is a Research Fellow at the Waseda University Institute of International Studies, Japan.</p>
